$$
\begin{array}{l} \underset {\theta} {\arg \min } - \sum_ {x, y} \log p (y \mid x; \theta) + \lambda \| \theta \| ^ {2} (16) \\ = \arg \min  _ {\theta} - \sum_ {x, y} \theta_ {y} ^ {\top} \phi (x) - \log \sum_ {y ^ {\prime}} \exp \left\{\theta_ {y ^ {\prime}} ^ {\top} \phi (x) \right\} + \lambda \| \theta \| ^ {2} (17) \\ \end{array}
$$

for each $\phi = \phi^1, \phi^2$ . As in Proposition 1, suppose the two feature functions produce similar predictors in the sense that:

$$
\mathbb {E} \left| p \left(y \mid x, \theta^ {1}\right) - p \left(y \mid x, \theta^ {2}\right) \right| <   \epsilon \tag {18}
$$

for all training $x$ . Then,

$$
\left| \theta_ {y, i} ^ {1} - \theta_ {y, i} ^ {2} \right| \leq \frac {\epsilon}{\lambda} \tag {19}
$$

Proof. Eq. (16) is convex, and at optimality its gradient with respect to each component of $\theta$ is 0. Then,

for any $\theta_{v,i}$ we have:

$$
0 = \sum_ {x, y = v} \phi_ {i} (x) (- 1 + p (y \mid x; \theta)) + \sum_ {x, y \neq v} \phi_ {i} (x) p (y \mid x; \theta) + \lambda \theta_ {v, i} \tag {20}
$$

$$
\theta_ {y, i} = \frac {1}{\lambda} \left(\sum_ {x, y = v} \phi_ {i} (x) (1 - p (y \mid x; \theta)) + \sum_ {x, y \neq v} - \phi_ {i} (x) p (y \mid x; \theta)\right) \tag {21}
$$

$$
\begin{array}{l} \left| \theta_ {y, i} ^ {1} - \theta_ {y, i} ^ {2} \right| = \frac {1}{\lambda} \sum_ {x, y} \phi_ {i} \left| p (y \mid x; \theta_ {1}) - p (y \mid x; \theta_ {2}) \right| (22) \\ \leq \frac {\epsilon}{\lambda} (23) \\ \end{array}
$$

